BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:[C3] LECTURE: Basic Concepts of Human Anatomy 1 & 2
DTSTART:20260902T150000Z
DTEND:20260902T165000Z
DTSTAMP:20260912T000418Z
UID:f5278e21-4432-400f-b7c1-f3660445fb84_E198667@calendar.med.ualberta.ca
DESCRIPTION:\nCourse: MED 511\n\nInstructors:\n	Karyne Rabey (rabey)\n\nMy
  Group:\n	Name: EDM\n	Location: KHRA 2-190\n\nObjectives:\n	Define and app
 ly the ‘Anatomical Position’ and explain its use in creating a uniform
  and universal terminology for describing the human body and its parts rel
 ative to one another anatomically & clinically.\n	Define the different Reg
 ions & Body Cavities with reference to their use in clinical medicine. Com
 pare and contrast the Regional & Systemic approaches to the study of human
  anatomy.\n	Describe the different Anatomical Planes and explain their use
  in defining points of reference for anatomical and clinical descriptions\
 , in particular with reference to physical examination\, surgical approach
 es\, imaging interpretation and directional terms.\n	Define and apply the 
 Positional Terms used specifically with reference to the extremities and t
 heir use in studying clinical anatomy and practicing medicine.\n	Define th
 e Terminology to describe Movements of the trunks and extremities.\n	Descr
 ibe the basic Anatomy of Skin\, the first structure encountered during dis
 sections\, physical examination and surgical interventions\, with special 
 reference to its protective functions and regenerative properties in wound
  healing.\n	Describe the role of the different structures found within ski
 n in its functions and understand the basic Anatomic Concepts at play duri
 ng wound repair and healing of burns.\n	Apply the anatomical and clinical 
 terminology and concepts learned in this lecture to Build an Organ of the 
 human body\, using the female breast as an example.\n	Define and apply the
  ‘Anatomical Position’ and explain its use in creating a uniform and u
 niversal terminology for describing the human body and its parts relative 
 to one another anatomically & clinically.\n	Define the different Regions &
  Body Cavities with reference to their use in clinical medicine. Compare a
 nd contrast the Regional & Systemic approaches to the study of human anato
 my.\n	Describe the different Anatomical Planes and explain their use in de
 fining points of reference for anatomical and clinical descriptions\, in p
 articular with reference to physical examination\, surgical approaches\, i
 maging interpretation and directional terms.\n	Define and apply the Positi
 onal Terms used specifically with reference to the extremities and their u
 se in studying clinical anatomy and practicing medicine.\n	Define the Term
 inology to describe Movements of the trunks and extremities.\n	Describe th
 e basic Anatomy of Skin\, the first structure encountered during dissectio
 ns\, physical examination and surgical interventions\, with special refere
 nce to its protective functions and regenerative properties in wound heali
 ng.\n	Describe the role of the different structures found within skin in i
 ts functions and understand the basic Anatomic Concepts at play during wou
 nd repair and healing of burns.\n	Apply the anatomical and clinical termin
 ology and concepts learned in this lecture to Build an Organ of the human 
 body\, using the female breast as an example.\n\nView this event online: h
 ttps://calendar.med.ualberta.ca/events/198667
LOCATION:
URL:https://calendar.med.ualberta.ca/events/198667
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:[C3] LECTURE: Structure & Function of Proteins
DTSTART:20260901T140000Z
DTEND:20260901T145000Z
DTSTAMP:20260912T000418Z
UID:f5278e21-4432-400f-b7c1-f3660445fb84_E198668@calendar.med.ualberta.ca
DESCRIPTION:\nCourse: MED 511\n\nInstructors:\n	Adrienne Wright (awright)\
 n\nMy Group:\n	Name: EDM\n	Location: KHRA 2-190\n\nObjectives:\n	Define th
 e term ‘protein’ and state what determines the primary structure of a 
 protein.\n	Explain how globular proteins fold and state how their structur
 e is stabilized.\n	Define the term prosthetic group and state why they are
  sometimes required in globular proteins.\n	Describe how the hydrophobic e
 ffect in protein folding and behaviour affects the clinical presentation o
 f sickle-cell anemia.\n	Outline the basis for prion diseases in biochemica
 l terms relating to protein structure.\n	State how fibrous proteins differ
  structurally from globular proteins.\n	Explain\, in biochemical terms\, h
 ow the structure of collagen relates to its function.\n	Apply the biochemi
 stry of proteins to common and severe clinical presentations.\n	State the 
 two key factors which determine how globular proteins ‘work’.\n	Explai
 n how proteins bind ligands specifically\, reversibly\, and with high affi
 nity.\n	Explain how the affinity of a protein for its ligand can vary.\n	D
 efine the term ‘protein’ and state what determines the primary structu
 re of a protein.\n	Explain how globular proteins fold and state how their 
 structure is stabilized.\n	Define the term prosthetic group and state why 
 they are sometimes required in globular proteins.\n	Describe how the hydro
 phobic effect in protein folding and behaviour affects the clinical presen
 tation of sickle-cell anemia.\n	Outline the basis for prion diseases in bi
 ochemical terms relating to protein structure.\n	State how fibrous protein
 s differ structurally from globular proteins.\n	Explain\, in biochemical t
 erms\, how the structure of collagen relates to its function.\n	Apply the 
 biochemistry of proteins to common and severe clinical presentations.\n	St
 ate the two key factors which determine how globular proteins ‘work’.\
 n	Explain how proteins bind ligands specifically\, reversibly\, and with h
 igh affinity.\n	Explain how the affinity of a protein for its ligand can v
 ary.\n\nView this event online: https://calendar.med.ualberta.ca/events/19
 8668
LOCATION:
URL:https://calendar.med.ualberta.ca/events/198668
END:VEVENT
