BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:[C3] LECTURE: Cell Metabolism 3 & 4
DTSTART:20261007T160000Z
DTEND:20261007T175000Z
DTSTAMP:20260912T000418Z
UID:f5278e21-4432-400f-b7c1-f3660445fb84_E198744@calendar.med.ualberta.ca
DESCRIPTION:\nCourse: MED 511\n\nInstructors:\n	Adrienne Wright (awright)\
 n\nMy Group:\n	Name: EDM\n	Location: 2-190 KHRA\n\nObjectives:\n	Describe 
 the difference between ketogenic and glucogenic amino acids.\n	Describe an
  important metabolic consequences of high protein diets.\n	Define the term
  adipocyte.\n	Describe what determines the rate of lipolysis (TAG breakdow
 n) in adipocytes.\n	State what determines the rate of TAG synthesis in adi
 pocytes.\n	Identify the major product of b-oxidation.\n	Describe the funct
 ion of ketone bodies and state where\, and from what\, they are made.\n	De
 scribe the origin of the carbon atoms in a newly synthesized fatty acid.\n
 	Discuss whether insulin and glucagon would activate or inhibit lipolysis 
 (in adipocytes) and fatty acid synthesis in the liver.\n	Describe why lipi
 ds must be transported in lipoprotein particles.\n	Describe the functions 
 of CM\, VLDL\, LDL and HDL.\n	Explain how eating sugar can lead to an incr
 eased rate of cholesterol synthesis in the liver.\n	Explain how the family
  of drugs known as “statins” reduce cholesterol synthesis.\n	Discuss w
 hy high cholesterol levels in the blood are a risk factor in the developme
 nt of atherosclerosis.\n	Explain\, in the context of their different metab
 olic roles\, why LDL is considered “bad” cholesterol and HDL is consid
 ered “good” cholesterol.\n	Describe the normal changes in glucose\, in
 sulin\, and glucagon levels in the blood\, during a normal\, daily\, fed-f
 asted cycle.\n	Identify sources of glucose in the bloodstream for an indiv
 idual in fed\, fasted\, early starved and late starved condition.\n	Identi
 fy the alternative fuel molecule which is produced in the latter stages of
  early starvation to conserve glucose for obligatory users.\n	Describe the
  difference between ketogenic and glucogenic amino acids.\n	Describe an im
 portant metabolic consequences of high protein diets.\n	Define the term ad
 ipocyte.\n	Describe what determines the rate of lipolysis (TAG breakdown) 
 in adipocytes.\n	State what determines the rate of TAG synthesis in adipoc
 ytes.\n	Identify the major product of b-oxidation.\n	Describe the function
  of ketone bodies and state where\, and from what\, they are made.\n	Descr
 ibe the origin of the carbon atoms in a newly synthesized fatty acid.\n	Di
 scuss whether insulin and glucagon would activate or inhibit lipolysis (in
  adipocytes) and fatty acid synthesis in the liver.\n	Describe why lipids 
 must be transported in lipoprotein particles.\n	Describe the functions of 
 CM\, VLDL\, LDL and HDL.\n	Explain how eating sugar can lead to an increas
 ed rate of cholesterol synthesis in the liver.\n	Explain how the family of
  drugs known as “statins” reduce cholesterol synthesis.\n	Discuss why 
 high cholesterol levels in the blood are a risk factor in the development 
 of atherosclerosis.\n	Explain\, in the context of their different metaboli
 c roles\, why LDL is considered “bad” cholesterol and HDL is considere
 d “good” cholesterol.\n	Describe the normal changes in glucose\, insul
 in\, and glucagon levels in the blood\, during a normal\, daily\, fed-fast
 ed cycle.\n	Identify sources of glucose in the bloodstream for an individu
 al in fed\, fasted\, early starved and late starved condition.\n	Identify 
 the alternative fuel molecule which is produced in the latter stages of ea
 rly starvation to conserve glucose for obligatory users.\n\nView this even
 t online: https://calendar.med.ualberta.ca/events/198744
LOCATION:
URL:https://calendar.med.ualberta.ca/events/198744
END:VEVENT
